WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.379 --> 00:00:05.744
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.744 --> 00:00:08.950
This is a picture of my
family from 20 years ago.

00:00:08.950 --> 00:00:11.943
I know what you're thinking,
I was a super cute kid.

00:00:11.943 --> 00:00:16.020
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; My mom is an incredible chef.

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:18.968
Every time I go home,
she makes all of my favorites,

00:00:18.968 --> 00:00:20.980
from springrolls to roasted pork.

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:27.820
Ever since I can remember, my family
ate dinner together every single night.

00:00:28.850 --> 00:00:31.710
You see, my parents wanted
some consistency in our lives.

00:00:32.740 --> 00:00:37.163
We moved six times before I
turned the age of 12, and

00:00:37.163 --> 00:00:41.405
as a shy kid,
I had a hard time speaking up in class.

00:00:41.405 --> 00:00:42.990
It was a different story
at the dinner table though.

00:00:44.070 --> 00:00:46.940
Over the years I made a number of updates,

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:51.534
such as how I got the leading role
of John Adams, yes, John Adams.

00:00:51.534 --> 00:00:54.481
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; In the 5th grade school play,

00:00:54.481 --> 00:00:56.630
to explain to them what prom was.

00:00:57.750 --> 00:01:01.533
And as the years went by,
my parents also gave their own updates.

00:01:01.533 --> 00:01:03.770
My parents talked about how
it was really difficult for

00:01:03.770 --> 00:01:07.180
them to immigrate to the United States,
having to leave our grandparents behind.

00:01:08.590 --> 00:01:13.280
And my brother also talked about how
middle school was tough and bullies,

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:14.580
they were the worst.

00:01:16.490 --> 00:01:19.090
We would eat, talk and reconnect.

00:01:20.630 --> 00:01:23.530
Time slowed down for us, and
it was my favorite part of the day.

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:27.927
Consistent family dinners
have numerous benefits.

00:01:27.927 --> 00:01:30.333
But as I think about the way
that we eat meals now,

00:01:30.333 --> 00:01:33.530
it makes me wonder if this timeless
tradition is disappearing.

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:40.890
As future leaders and future parents,
it is our responsibility to save this art.

00:01:42.830 --> 00:01:46.172
The benefits of family dinner
have been studied for decades.

00:01:46.172 --> 00:01:48.750
Columbia University found that
children who ate at least

00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:52.870
five times a week with their parents were
more likely to do well in school and

00:01:52.870 --> 00:01:55.610
four times less likely to smoke.

00:01:55.610 --> 00:02:00.374
What they also found was that these same
kids were more liking to say that their

00:02:00.374 --> 00:02:02.133
parents were proud of them,

00:02:02.133 --> 00:02:05.605
that their parents are the people
that they confide to.

00:02:05.605 --> 00:02:06.848
In just 30 minutes a day,

00:02:06.848 --> 00:02:10.210
parents were able to foster stronger
relationships with their parents.

00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:15.001
How many of you in this
room are current parents or

00:02:15.001 --> 00:02:16.793
want to be parents in the future?

00:02:19.105 --> 00:02:21.197
So most of the room.

00:02:21.197 --> 00:02:25.010
So what's the problem, we have to eat
anyways, so why not eat together?

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:31.170
And even if you don't have a family just
yet, you can go home to your parents,

00:02:31.170 --> 00:02:33.720
to your roommates, to your loved ones,
to your chosen family.

00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:39.010
The problem though is that
the way that we eat meals now

00:02:39.010 --> 00:02:42.330
contrasts with the fundamentals
of a family dinner.

00:02:42.330 --> 00:02:43.420
And there are three reasons why.

00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:47.990
The first is that we're
used to eating alone now.

00:02:49.470 --> 00:02:53.602
Dinner has become
a nutritional transaction.

00:02:53.602 --> 00:02:57.640
We can DoorDash anything we want,
have it in 30 minutes and

00:02:57.640 --> 00:02:59.393
eat it on our couch alone.

00:02:59.393 --> 00:03:03.941
In fact, 50% of meals are now eaten alone.

00:03:03.941 --> 00:03:08.510
I get it, it's been a long day,
all you want to do is relax.

00:03:10.270 --> 00:03:13.281
But are meals the time for us to do so?

00:03:13.281 --> 00:03:15.819
And when we have families
will we all eat alone so

00:03:15.819 --> 00:03:17.860
that we can recharge independently?

00:03:20.150 --> 00:03:24.680
It's not just eating alone though,
distractions are now everywhere.

00:03:25.750 --> 00:03:29.002
Smartphones have given us access
to information in seconds, but

00:03:29.002 --> 00:03:32.205
they've also given us an excuse
not to engage with one another.

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:37.120
The Huffington Post asked millennials,
what does meal time mean to you?

00:03:38.210 --> 00:03:40.469
And we responded with
it's a time to multitask.

00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:44.440
We can watch Netflix,
we can catch up on emails.

00:03:45.970 --> 00:03:49.875
A family dinner requires
that all of us are present.

00:03:49.875 --> 00:03:53.161
If you're not there to talk about
something, you're there to listen and

00:03:53.161 --> 00:03:53.750
to support.

00:03:55.330 --> 00:03:57.840
How can we do that if we're
constantly glued to our devices?

00:04:00.050 --> 00:04:05.830
And if that wasn't enough, work-life
balance is just hard to achieve nowadays.

00:04:05.830 --> 00:04:07.765
I mean, we're in Silicon Valley,

00:04:07.765 --> 00:04:11.777
we all know to well the perks of working
at our neighborhood tech company.

00:04:11.777 --> 00:04:15.030
Three comp meals a day,
unlimited snack bars.

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:20.830
From that weekly road warrior to that
employee who stays just a little bit

00:04:20.830 --> 00:04:26.030
longer to squeeze out that extra meal and
do a little bit more work.

00:04:26.030 --> 00:04:31.482
Our jobs have a way of
influencing how we end our days.

00:04:31.482 --> 00:04:34.150
I mean, we're individuals now,
so we can make a decision.

00:04:35.170 --> 00:04:36.990
But what happens when we have families?

00:04:38.210 --> 00:04:42.180
Will you be willing to leave on time every
single night to get home to your family so

00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:43.090
you can have dinner together?

00:04:45.130 --> 00:04:47.770
Every generation wants more,
more time, more speed,

00:04:47.770 --> 00:04:51.810
more convenience,
how will we defend the family dinner?

00:04:53.940 --> 00:04:58.190
I'm looking at the answer right now,
I'm looking at future leaders.

00:04:59.290 --> 00:05:02.590
As we've seen,
policies can have really great benefits,

00:05:02.590 --> 00:05:04.499
but a lot of unintended side effects.

00:05:06.210 --> 00:05:10.850
When Uber was reviewed by Eric Holder,
one of the first things that he changed

00:05:10.850 --> 00:05:14.710
was he moved the company catered
dinner time from 8:15 to 7 PM.

00:05:15.710 --> 00:05:17.990
He wanted it to signal the end of the day.

00:05:19.595 --> 00:05:24.760
YouTube's CEO makes it a point to have
dinner with her family every single night.

00:05:24.760 --> 00:05:27.440
She says,
I try because if I can make it home for

00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:30.300
dinner, I can get the scoop
from my kids on their day.

00:05:31.670 --> 00:05:36.110
We have policies like maternity leave and
paternity leave for new parents.

00:05:36.110 --> 00:05:38.720
We should also have policies that
promote family time when those new

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:40.190
parents return to work.

00:05:41.560 --> 00:05:46.152
As future leaders, we have
the opportunity to prioritize family

00:05:46.152 --> 00:05:51.003
dinners within our organizations,
so let's do it with intention.

00:05:51.003 --> 00:05:56.333
You're not just future leaders though,
you're also future parents.

00:05:56.333 --> 00:05:59.110
Parents set the traditions
at the family dinner table.

00:06:01.100 --> 00:06:05.902
Time Magazine did a study where they
compared those with devices at the table

00:06:05.902 --> 00:06:07.636
to those without devices.

00:06:07.636 --> 00:06:12.382
Those without devices, they enjoyed
their meals more, less distracted and

00:06:12.382 --> 00:06:13.340
more engaged.

00:06:14.800 --> 00:06:16.770
My mom worked a full-time job and

00:06:16.770 --> 00:06:18.940
she committed three hours a day
to make ends meet for us.

00:06:20.060 --> 00:06:25.250
Yet every night when she came home we ate
dinner together without distractions.

00:06:26.550 --> 00:06:29.301
I am forever grateful to her for that, and

00:06:29.301 --> 00:06:32.691
I want to see a family dinner
at every single table.

00:06:34.831 --> 00:06:40.274
I was reading a magazine a few weeks ago,
and I came across this quote,

00:06:40.274 --> 00:06:44.810
to eat is a necessity, but
to eat intelligently is an art.

00:06:46.380 --> 00:06:49.205
What does he mean by intelligently,
is it a keto thing?

00:06:49.205 --> 00:06:54.192
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; For me, to eat intelligently is

00:06:54.192 --> 00:06:59.260
to move beyond eating alone to
actually gathering at the table.

00:07:00.450 --> 00:07:05.690
To move beyond our everyday distractions
and to being present for one another.

00:07:07.030 --> 00:07:13.596
To move beyond just having
a meal to having a conversation.

00:07:13.596 --> 00:07:20.260
For me, to eat is a necessity,
but to eat together is an art.

00:07:22.300 --> 00:07:24.450
I recently returned home for
the holidays, and

00:07:24.450 --> 00:07:28.150
like clockwork at 6:30,
my parents called us down for dinner.

00:07:29.730 --> 00:07:33.780
As you can see, my mom still
makes all of my favorite dishes.

00:07:35.580 --> 00:07:39.230
Over our meal, my brother told us how he
had been selected to be in a recruiting

00:07:39.230 --> 00:07:39.929
video at work.

00:07:41.920 --> 00:07:44.840
My parents asked us when we could
take our family portraits together.

00:07:46.630 --> 00:07:50.976
I shared that I would be
giving this low keynote.

00:07:50.976 --> 00:07:55.912
The clinking of the dishes,
my brother's cackling laugh,

00:07:55.912 --> 00:07:59.310
I sat there in bliss, so happy to be home.

00:08:01.320 --> 00:08:07.872
Let's bring dinner back where it belongs,
together and at the table.

00:08:07.872 --> 00:08:10.639
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:08:10.639 --> 00:08:15.443
[MUSIC]

